node_modules/
dist/
# Both of these were excluded in .git/info/exclude, which is local to one disk and
# never cloned. The directories are nested git repositories with their own
# remotes, so no content was at risk - but the decision to keep them out of this
# repo lived in exactly one place, and a fresh clone would not have inherited it.
refer-zo-bootstrap/
.claude/worktrees/
# VS Code Local History. It snapshots whatever is open, including files that are
# ignored everywhere else, and it was tracked here rather than ignored - so on
# 2026-06-17 the commit named "chore: repo safeguard snapshot" pushed two
# alliance-hub/.dev.vars snapshots to a PUBLIC repo. What those two held was mild
# (a local placeholder secret and one phone number). What they would hold today
# is not: that same file now carries SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, OPEN_AI_CHAT_KEY
# and ALLIANCE_SMS_RELAY_TOKEN.
#
# The .env* rules below never covered it, because a .dev.vars is not a .env. The
# hole was never the pattern list - it was that an editor was allowed to choose
# what enters the repo.
.history/
alliance-hub/.deploy/
alliance-hub/.wrangler/
*.vsix
.DS_Store
npm-debug.log*
.env
.env.*
.env*
.refer-factory/*.local.json
.refer-factory/process-state.json
# The pulse belt is universal factory state, rewritten every tick by the one
# machine that owns it. Registries under .refer-factory are tracked; a heartbeat
# is not a registry, and "dirty has to mean something".
.refer-factory/pulse-belt.jsonl
.refer-factory/pulse-belt.json
.refer-factory/updates/
zo-computer/usage/*.jsonl
# Same class as the line above, in the other directory it was written to. Nothing
# in this repo has written it since 2026-05-01.
usage/*.jsonl
# Chromium crash noise from an Electron host, 316 bytes of it, tracked since
# April. npm-debug.log* above did not match the name.
debug.log
